WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.229 --> 00:00:05.910
Let's look at the Twinmotion UI and learn to navigate in the viewport.

2
00:00:06.599 --> 00:00:12.140
To that purpose, we'll use the 01-context project file that has been provided to you.

3
00:00:12.660 --> 00:00:16.199
It's essentially the same setup that was used in the last video,

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.800
except that the data has been imported as a Datasmith file instead of using a direct link.

5
00:00:22.059 --> 00:00:26.559
Normally, when you start Twinmotion, you are greeted with a Home page,

6
00:00:26.820 --> 00:00:30.300
which you can also access at any time using the Home icon.

7
00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:35.719
This page offers you some news, quick access to recent projects,

8
00:00:36.350 --> 00:00:39.350
a Learning tab to get up to speed on Twinmotion,

9
00:00:39.939 --> 00:00:44.539
and a Templates page to help you start a project based on your line of work.

10
00:00:45.229 --> 00:00:49.229
These templates are downloadable so you can download only those that you need.

11
00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:55.880
The Home page automatically shows a startup unless you disable that option.

12
00:00:57.369 --> 00:00:58.770
Close the Home page.

13
00:00:59.259 --> 00:01:04.459
Let's look at viewport navigation and then move on to explore the various UI components.

14
00:01:05.640 --> 00:01:11.140
For the most part, Twinmotion uses the same navigation tools that you have in Unreal Engine,

15
00:01:11.769 --> 00:01:18.269
and by default, a navigation panel is displayed at the top of the viewport to remind you of those hotkeys.

16
00:01:18.870 --> 00:01:22.969
For example, you can use the right mouse click to look around,

17
00:01:27.379 --> 00:01:30.579
the AWSD keys to walk around,

18
00:01:31.840 --> 00:01:39.739
Furthermore, you can alter the camera speed by using the 1-6 keys, going from slower to faster movement.

19
00:01:41.819 --> 00:01:50.340
The mouse wheel on its own is used for panning or to orbit around when used with the Shift key.

20
00:01:53.079 --> 00:01:59.780
You can do some minimal adjustments using the Settings icon to set the hotkeys to mimic your DCC app.

21
00:02:01.079 --> 00:02:09.840
For example, setting the hotkeys to 3ds Max forces the orbit to use Alt and mouse wheel instead of Shift.

22
00:02:11.270 --> 00:02:17.969
I personally like to set the key mapping to Unreal Engine mode for consistency as I use both products.

23
00:02:20.330 --> 00:02:26.129
Once you are comfortable with the settings you have chosen, you can hide the navigation panel altogether.

24
00:02:27.490 --> 00:02:38.389
If you need to recall it, you can do so from the Eye menu which gives you faster access to common tools

25
00:02:38.889 --> 00:02:46.560
such as Time of Day or Camera Speed if you forget about the hotkeys.

26
00:03:00.629 --> 00:03:08.030
Another simple UI component is the Maximize-Minimize Viewport toggle that does exactly as the name implies.

27
00:03:09.939 --> 00:03:16.939
Other than these two viewport icons we just discussed, there are four more areas that make the Twinmotion interface.

28
00:03:17.729 --> 00:03:28.639
There's a toolbar that gives you access to transform tools, move, rotate, scale, as well as a material picker to sample the materials off objects.

29
00:03:29.240 --> 00:03:34.340
There is also a button to activate Path Tracing render mode if your hardware permits it.

30
00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:45.000
The toolbar can be expanded to give you access to further functionality like local transforms and pivot point adjustments.

31
00:03:46.849 --> 00:03:54.340
Two more UI elements are rather prominent, the asset library to the left and the scene graph to the right.

32
00:03:55.740 --> 00:04:03.240
The asset library as the name implies contains a plethora of ready-made assets that you can add to the scene using a simple drag and drop.

33
00:04:03.539 --> 00:04:13.360
You can easily navigate through the various asset categories and sub-categories or search by keywords.

34
00:04:20.939 --> 00:04:25.540
The scene graph on the other hand displays the asset data and hierarchies in your scene.

35
00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:30.670
You can use it to select scene objects and control their visibility.

36
00:04:36.579 --> 00:04:39.680
The Ambience section of the scene graph is important.

37
00:04:40.079 --> 00:04:44.480
It doesn't relate to an asset in the scene but rather to the environment as a whole.

38
00:04:45.500 --> 00:04:49.399
It lets you adjust global lighting parameters based on the time of day.

39
00:04:50.959 --> 00:04:55.060
It lets you adjust exposure with full manual control if you need it.

40
00:04:58.920 --> 00:05:02.920
You can set the weather and seasons to your liking.

41
00:05:10.209 --> 00:05:15.509
You can adjust the North direction, date and location anywhere in the world.

42
00:05:17.389 --> 00:05:26.040
You can use a custom background including an HDRI panorama or a simple background which we'll experiment with later.

43
00:05:27.370 --> 00:05:32.269
You can even enable an ocean effect if you are simulating certain areas of the world.

44
00:05:32.769 --> 00:05:38.269
The right side of the screen is in fact made of different panels that you can toggle on or off.

45
00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:45.660
The Scene Graph and Properties panel are rather prominent, but you also have access to a Transforms panel.

46
00:05:46.569 --> 00:05:50.670
This panel will give you better control over the placement of objects in the scene.

47
00:05:51.910 --> 00:05:56.009
There is also a View Sets panel which we'll not be discussing in this class.

48
00:05:56.860 --> 00:05:59.459
At the bottom of your screen is the Dock.

49
00:06:00.029 --> 00:06:04.629
A series of five panels that you use to control various aspects of the scene.

50
00:06:05.319 --> 00:06:07.920
You already know a bit about the Import panel.

51
00:06:09.120 --> 00:06:14.519
The Materials panel shows you the materials used in the scene, including the imported ones.

52
00:06:15.120 --> 00:06:20.519
The Populate option opens a panel on the right that gives you access to more creation techniques.

53
00:06:21.339 --> 00:06:24.939
This is where you access the Foliage tool to scatter vegetation.

54
00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:30.129
It also gives you access to animation tools based on path motions.

55
00:06:30.529 --> 00:06:33.329
Again, you will experiment with these shortly.

56
00:06:34.209 --> 00:06:39.410
But you also have access to an Urban tool which lets you add context to your project.

57
00:06:39.709 --> 00:06:41.209
By adding surroundings.

58
00:06:41.910 --> 00:06:48.110
This Urban tool will be the main topic that we'll investigate next, but let's keep moving on with the UI.

59
00:06:48.639 --> 00:06:51.939
The last two panels in the Dock are about outputting data.

60
00:06:52.920 --> 00:07:03.149
The Media panel is where you define still images, videos or panoramas that you plan to produce.

61
00:07:03.910 --> 00:07:12.009
Once you set them up in a satisfactory way, you use the last panel, the Export panel, to output them to disk.

62
00:07:12.439 --> 00:07:17.069
The project was imported nicely into Twinmotion, but it looks a little bare.

63
00:07:17.740 --> 00:07:23.040
In the next step, you add context to the Community Center in the form of surrounding buildings.
